5/2, d_y=1 +1197\u221a14 /200.\n\nSo, plug in:\n\n- (5/2)(20\u221a14 +5/2) - (1197\u221a14 /200)(1 +1197\u221a14 /200 ) = -100\u221a14.\n\nCompute each part:\n\nFirst term: - (5/2)(20\u221a14) - (5/2)(5/2)= -50\u221a14 -25/4.\n\nSecond term: - (1197\u221a14 /200)*1 - (1197\u221a14 /200)*(1197\u221a14 /200 )= -1197\u221a14 /200 - (1197\u00b2 *14 ) / (200\u00b2 ).\n\nSo, equation becomes:\n\n-50\u221a14 -25/4 -1197\u221a14 /200 - (1,432,809*14)/40,000 = -100\u221a14.\n\nWait, 1,432,809*14=20,062,526.\n\nThus, equation:\n\n-50\u221a14 -25/4 -1197\u221a14 /200 -20,062,526 /40,000 = -100\u221a14.\n\nBring all terms to left-hand side:\n\n-50\u221a14 -25/4 -1197\u221a14 /200 -20,062,526 /40,000 +100\u221a14=0.\n\nCombine like terms:\n\n(-50\u221a14 +100\u221a14) + (-25/4) + (-1197\u221a14 /200) + (-20,062,526 /40,000)=0.\n\nSo, 50\u221a14 -25/4 -1197\u221a14 /200 -20,062,526 /40,000=0.\n\nExpress 50\u221a14 as (50*200\u221a14)/200=10,000\u221a14 /200.\n\nSo, 10,000\u221a14 /200 -1197\u221a14 /200= (10,000 -1197)/200 \u221a14=8,803\u221a14 /200.\n\nSimilarly, -25/4 -20,062,526 /40,000.\n\nConvert -25/4 to -25*10,000 /40,000= -250,000 /40,000.\n\nThus, -250,000 /40,000 -20,062,526 /40,000= (-250,000 -20,062,526)/40,000= -20,312,526 /40,000.\n\nSimplify: -20,312,526 /40,000= -20,312,526 \u00f72 /20,000= -10,156,263 /20,000.\n\nThus, equation becomes:\n\n8,000\u221a14 /200 -10,156,263 /20,000=0.\n\nWait, no:\n\nWait, 8,000\u221a14 /200=40\u221a14.\n\n-10,156,263 /20,000= -505,813.15 /1000.\n\nWait, maybe not.\n\nWait, perhaps it's better to compute the decimal values.\n\nCompute 8,000\u221a14 /200=40\u221a14\u224840*3.7417\u2248149.268.\n\n-10,156,263 /20,000\u2248-505,813.15 /1,000\u2248-505.81315.\n\nThus, equation is approximately 149.268 -505.81315\u2248-356.545.\n\nBut left side is -356.545\u22480? That can't be.\n\nWait, perhaps made a miscalculation.\n\nWait, let me recompute:\n\nFrom equation:\n\n50\u221a14 /1 -25/1 -1197\u221a14 /2000 -20,062,526 /40,000=0.\n\nWait, no, perhaps I made a mistake in simplifying.\n\nWait, original equation after combining terms:\n\n50\u221a14 -25/1 -1197\u221a14 /200 -20,062,526 /40,000=0.\n\nLet me compute each term:\n\n50\u221a14\u224850*3.7417\u2248186.085.\n\n-25.\n\n-1197\u221a14 /200\u2248-1197*3.7417 /200\u2248-1197*0.018685\u2248-22.28.\n\n-20,062,526 /40,000\u2248-501.56565.\n\nThus, total\u2248186.085 -25 -22.28 -501.56565\u2248186.085 -25=161.085; 161.085 -22.28\u2248138.805; 138.805 -501.56565\u2248-362.76\u22480. Not possible.\n\nHmm, seems like this approach isn't working.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the initial assumption is wrong.\n\nWait, perhaps I made an error in computing the polar line.\n\nWait, maybe it's better to use another approach.\n\nGiven that AD intersects \u03c9 again at P, and we need to find AP.\n\nGiven triangle ABC with sides AB=5, BC=9, AC=10.\n\nFirst, note that triangle with sides 5,9,10 is not a right triangle.\n\nCompute semi-perimeter: s=(5+9+10)/2=12.\n\nArea using Heron's formula: sqrt[s(s-a)(s-b)(s-c)]=sqrt[12*7*3*2]=sqrt[1008]=12*sqrt(7).\n\nThus, area is 12\u221a7.\n\nCompute circumradius R= (a*b*c)/(4*area)= (5*9*10)/(4*12\u221a7)= (450)/(48\u221a7)= (75)/(8\u221a7)=75\u221a7/56.\n\nThus, R=75\u221a7 /56.\n\nNow, AD is the symmedian from A.\n\nWait, because D is the intersection of tangents at B and C, so D is the exsimilicenter of the circumcircle.\n\nWait, the symmedian is the reflection of the median over the angle bisector.\n\nAlternatively, since D is the ex-tangent point, then AD is the symmedian.\n\nThus, AP is the length from A to the second intersection point of AD with \u03c9.\n\nIn a triangle, the length from a vertex to the second intersection point of a symmedian can be found using properties of symmedian.\n\nAlternatively, since AD is the symmedian, the ratio of AP to PD can be found via symmedian properties.\n\nWait, the symmedian divides the opposite side in the ratio of the squares of the adjacent sides.\n\nWait, but here it's the symmedian from A, so it would divide BC in the ratio AB\u00b2 to AC\u00b2.\n\nBut in this case, we have a circumcircle, so perhaps power of point.\n\nWait, another approach: Use coordinates.\n\nLet me place triangle ABC in coordinate plane.\n\nLet me place point A at (0,0), point B at (5,0), and point C somewhere in the plane.\n\nGiven sides AB=5, BC=9, AC=10.\n\nSo, coordinates:\n\nA=(0,0), B=(5,0). Let point C=(x,y).\n\nCompute distances:\n\nAC=10: x\u00b2 + y\u00b2=100.\n\nBC=9: (x-5)\u00b2 + y\u00b2=81.\n\nSubtract equations: (x-5)\u00b2 + y\u00b2 - (x\u00b2 + y\u00b2)=81 -100= -19.\n\nExpand: x\u00b2 -10x +25 + y\u00b2 -x\u00b2 - y\u00b2= -10x +25= -19.\n\nThus, -10x= -44, so x=44/10=22/5=4.4.\n\nThus, x=22/5.\n\nThen, x\u00b2 + y\u00b2=100: (22/5)^2 + y\u00b2=100.\n\nCompute (22/5)^2=484/25.\n\nThus, y\u00b2=100 -484/25=2500/25 -484/25=2016/25.\n\nThus, y= sqrt(2016/25)= (sqrt(2016))/5= (12*sqrt(14))/5.\n\nThus, point C is at (22/5, (12\u221a14)/5).\n\nNow, find the equation of the circumcircle \u03c9.\n\nPoints A=(0,0), B=(5,0), C=(22/5,12\u221a14/5).\n\nGeneral equation of circle: x\u00b2 + y\u00b2 + Dx + Ey + F=0.\n\nPlug in A: 0 +0 +0 +0 +F=0 \u21d2 F=0.\n\nPlug in B: 25 +0 +5D +0 +0=0 \u21d225 +5D=0 \u21d2D= -5.\n\nPlug in C: (22/5)^2 + (12\u221a14/5)^2 + (-5)(22/5) + E*(12\u221a14/5)=0.\n\nCompute:\n\n(484/25) + (2016/25) - (110/5) + (12\u221a14 E)/5=0.\n\nSimplify:\n\n(484 +2016)/25 -22 + (12\u221a14 E)/5=0.\n\n2500/25=100; 100 -22=78.\n\nThus, 78 + (12\u221a14 E)/5=0 \u21d2 (12\u221a14 E)/5= -78 \u21d2 E= (-78*5)/(12\u221a14)= (-390)/(12\u221a14)= (-65)/(2\u221a14)= (-65\u221a14)/(28).\n\nThus, equation of \u03c9: x\u00b2 + y\u00b2 -5x - (65\u221a14)/28 y=0.\n\nNow, find the equation of tangents at B and C.\n\nThe tangent at a point (x1,y1) on circle x\u00b2 + y\u00b2 + Dx + Ey + F=0 is xx1 + yy1 + D(x +x1)/2 + E(y + y1)/2 + F=0.\n\nWait, alternative formula: The tangent at (x1,y1) is T=0, where T: x x1 + y y1 + D*(x +x1)/2 + E*(y + y1)/2 + F=0.\n\nWait, maybe