ominator: 56*5=280.\n\nSo, 10920/280= 39.\n\nThus, fourth term is (-65\u221a14 y)/56 -39.\n\nThus, putting all together:\n\n(22/5)x + (12\u221a14 /5)y -5x/2 -5*(22/5)/5 - (65\u221a14 /56)y -39=0.\n\nWait, no:\n\nWait, the third term is (-5/2)x - (5*(22)/5)/2.\n\nWait, no, let's correct:\n\nThird term is (-5/2)x - (5*(22/5))/2.\n\nWait, no, actually, the third term is:\n\nThird term: (-5/2)x - (5*(22/5))/2.\n\nWait, that's incorrect.\n\nWait, original expression:\n\nThird term is (-5/2)x - (5*(22/5))/2.\n\nWait, no:\n\nWait, D= -5, so:\n\n(-5)*(x +22/5)/2= (-5x/2 - (5*(22/5))/2)= (-5x/2 -22/2)= (-5x/2 -11).\n\nWait, yes, that's correct.\n\nSimilarly, E= -65\u221a14 /28, so:\n\nFourth term is (-65\u221a14 /28)*(y +12\u221a14 /5)/2= (-65\u221a14 /56)*(y +12\u221a14 /5)= (-65\u221a14 y /56 - (65\u221a14 *12\u221a14)/(56*5)).\n\nWhich is (-65\u221a14 y /56 - (65*12*14)/(56*5)).\n\nAs above, that's (-65\u221a14 y /56 - 156/5* something? Wait, no.\n\nWait, (65*12*14)/(56*5)= (65*12*14)/(280)= same as before 10920/280=39.\n\nThus, fourth term is (-65\u221a14 y)/56 -39.\n\nSo, putting all together:\n\nFirst term: (22/5)x\n\nSecond term: (12\u221a14 /5)y\n\nThird term: (-5x/2 -11)\n\nFourth term: (-65\u221a14 y /56 -39)\n\nThus, entire equation:\n\n(22/5)x + (12\u221a14 /5)y -5x/2 -11 -65\u221a14 y /56 -39=0.\n\nCombine like terms:\n\nx terms:\n\n(22/5)x - (5/2)x.\n\nConvert to common denominator 10:\n\n(44/10)x - (25/10)x= (19/10)x.\n\ny terms:\n\n(12\u221a14 /5)y - (65\u221a14 /56)y.\n\nConvert to common denominator 280:\n\n(12\u221a14 /5)= (12\u221a14 *56)/280= (672\u221a14)/280.\n\n(65\u221a14 /56)= (65\u221a14 *5)/280= (325\u221a14)/280.\n\nThus, difference: (672\u221a14 -325\u221a14)/280= (347\u221a14)/280.\n\nConstant terms:\n\n-11 -39= -50.\n\nThus, equation is:\n\n(19/10)x + (347\u221a14 /280)y -50=0.\n\nMultiply all terms by 280 to eliminate denominators:\n\n(19/10 *280)x +347\u221a14 y -50*280=0.\n\nCompute:\n\n19/10*280=19*28=532.\n\n50*280=14,000.\n\nThus, equation is:\n\n532x +347\u221a14 y -14,000=0.\n\nThus, tangent at C is 532x +347\u221a14 y =14,000.\n\nSimilarly, tangent at B.\n\nWait, maybe it's easier to compute equation for tangent at B.\n\nPoint B is (5,0).\n\nWait, no, point B is (5,0). Wait, in our coordinate system, point A is (0,0), point B is (5,0), point C is (22/5, 12\u221a14 /5). Wait, actually, no.\n\nWait, no, earlier, when computing point C, we had point C as (22/5, 12\u221a14 /5). Wait, actually, point C is (22/5, (12\u221a14)/5). So, point B is (5,0).\n\nWait, no, hold on, in the coordinate system, point A is (0,0), point B is (5,0), point C is (22/5, 12\u221a14 /5). So, point B is (5,0).\n\nWait, then tangent at B is the line tangent to \u03c9 at (5,0). Similarly, tangent at C is tangent at (22/5,12\u221a14 /5).\n\nSo, to compute the equations of these tangents.\n\nThe equation of tangent at a point (x0,y0) on circle \u03c9: x x0 + y y0 + D x + E y + F=0. Wait, no, more accurately, since the circle is x\u00b2 + y\u00b2 + D x + E y + F=0.\n\nBut in our case, the circle \u03c9 is x\u00b2 + y\u00b2 -5x - (65\u221a14 /28)y=0? Wait, no.\n\nWait, earlier, when we computed the equation of the circle, it was x\u00b2 + y\u00b2 + D x + E y + F=0, where D=-5, E= -65\u221a14 /28, F=0?\n\nWait, no, when we plugged in point A (0,0), which is on the circle, the equation is x\u00b2 + y\u00b2 -5x - (65\u221a14 /28)y=0.\n\nSo, the general equation is x\u00b2 + y\u00b2 -5x - (65\u221a14 /28)y=0.\n\nThus, the tangent at (x0,y0) on this circle is x x0 + y y0 -5*(x +x0)/2 - (65\u221a14 /28)*(y + y0)/2=0.\n\nWait, maybe it's better to use the formula for tangent at a point.\n\nWait, the equation of tangent to circle at point (x0,y0) is T: x x0 + y y0 + D*(x +x0)/2 + E*(y + y0)/2 + F=0.\n\nWait, since our circle is x\u00b2 + y\u00b2 + D x + E y + F=0, with D=-5, E= -65\u221a14 /28, F=0.\n\nThus, tangent at (x0,y0) is x x0 + y y0 + D*(x +x0)/2 + E*(y + y0)/2 + F=0.\n\nSo, plug in D=-5, E= -65\u221a14 /28, F=0.\n\nThus, tangent at (x0,y0) is x x0 + y y0 -5*(x +x0)/2 - (65\u221a14 /28)*(y + y0)/2=0.\n\nSo, for point B=(5,0):\n\nTangent at B is x*5 + y*0 -5*(x +5)/2 - (65\u221a14 /28)*(y +0)/2=0.\n\nSimplify:\n\n5x - (5x +25)/2 - (65\u221a14 y)/56=0.\n\nMultiply all terms by 56 to eliminate denominators:\n\n5x*56 - (5x +25)*28 -65\u221a14 y=0.\n\nCompute:\n\n5x*56=280x.\n\n(5x +25)*28=140x +700.\n\nThus, 280x -140x -700 -65\u221a14 y=0.\n\nSimplify:\n\n140x -700 -65\u221a14 y=0.\n\nDivide all terms by 65:\n\n(140/65)x - (700/65) -\u221a14 y=0.\n\nSimplify:\n\n140/65=28/13.\n\n700/65=140/13.\n\nThus, 28/13 x -140/13 -\u221a14 y=0.\n\nMultiply all terms by 13:\n\n28x -140 -13\u221a14 y=0.\n\nThus, tangent at B:28x -13\u221a14 y=140.\n\nSimilarly, tangent at C.\n\nPoint C is (22/5, 12\u221a14 /5).\n\nSo, tangent at C is x*(22/5) + y*(12\u221a14 /5) -5*(x +22/5)/2 - (65\u221a14 /28)*(y +12\u221a14 /5)/2=0.\n\nWait, no, using the formula:\n\nx x0 + y y0 -5*(x +x0)/2 - (65\u221a14 /28)*(y + y0)/2=0.\n\nSo, plug in x0=22/5, y0=12\u221a14 /5.\n\nCompute each term:\n\nx*(22/5) + y*(12\u221a14 /5) -5*(x +22/5)/2 - (65\u221a14 /28)*(y +12\u221a14 /5)/2=0.\n\nLet me compute term by term:\n\nFirst term: (22/5)x.\n\nSecond term: (12\u221a14 /5)y.\n\nThird term: -5*(x +22/5)/2= -5x/2 - (5*22/5)/2= -5x/2 -11.\n\nFourth term: -(65\u221a14 /28)*(y +12\u221a14 /5)/2= -(65\u221a14 /28)*(y)/2 - (65\u221a14 /28)*(12\u221a14 /5)/2.\n\nCompute each part:\n\nFirst part: -(65\u221a14 /56)y.\n\nSecond part: -(65\u221a14 *12\u221a14)/(28*5*2)= Let's compute numerator and denominator.\n\nNumerator: 65*12*(\u221a14)^2=65*12*14=65*168=10920.\n\nDenominator:28*5*2=280.\n\nThus, second part: -10920 /280= -10920 \u00f7 280= -39.\n\nThus, the fourth term is -(65\u221a14 /56)y -39.\n\nPutting it all together:\n\n(22/5)x + (12\u221a14 /5)y -5x/2 -11 - (65\u221a14 /56)y -39=0.\n\nCombine like terms:\n\nx terms: (22/5)x - (5/2)x.\n\nConvert to common denominator, which is 10:\n\n(44/10)x - (25/10)x= (19/10)x.\n\ny terms: (12\u